   236	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   237	// Inverse: Math s-expression -> karva (head, tail)
   238	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   239	
   240	/// A parsed Math node (intermediate tree between the s-expression and karva).
   241	#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
   242	enum MathNode {
   243	    Num(f64),
   244	    Var(String),
   245	    /// (constructor, children)
   246	    App(String, Vec<MathNode>),
   247	}
   248	
   249	/// Reverse of `semantic_to_math`: Math constructor name -> semantic id. Returns
   250	/// None for constructors with no single-token karva representation (none such
   251	/// in the current set).
   252	fn math_ctor_to_semantic(ctor: &str) -> Option<&'static str> {
   253	    Some(match ctor {
   254	        "Add" => "add",
   255	        "Sub" => "sub",
   256	        "Mul" => "mul",
   257	        "Div" => "div",
   258	        "Neg" => "neg",
   259	        "Sin" => "sin",
   260	        "Cos" => "cos",
   180	- **Per call:** `expressions`, `caller_facts`.
   181	- `rows` is the resident buffer the evaluator already holds.
   182	- `inputs` is **required**, never defaulted. An input named `c` was read as
   183	  the speed of light three separate times before this was made an argument
   184	  everywhere (`_resolve_rnc`, `concretize`, `fold_constant_subtrees`).
   185	- **The function body contains no arithmetic.** What `ProtectedDiv` means
   186	  lives in the evaluator's `semantic_id` -> kernel-case map (K5) and in the rules written against it.
   187	  Different tables, different linter; no code change.
   188	
   189	## 3. Layout (BUILT for evaluation)
   190	
   191	Level-order node arrays, as `gpu_eval::ExprBatch`: `nodes[]`, `offsets[]`,
   192	`lengths[]`; children located by a prefix sum over arities. Every child sits at
   193	a higher index than its parent, so one backward scan visits children first.
   194	
   195	Constraint: a rewritten form must decode into its gene's head length or it is
   196	dropped and counted (`n_oversized`, BUILT in `denoise_karva_candidates_batch`).
   197	
   198	## 4. Kernels
   199	
   200	| # | Kernel | Work item | Status |
   201	|---|---|---|---|
   202	| K1 | guard facts | node (backward scan) → fact bitmask | DESIGNED |
   203	| K2 | match | expression × position × rule → hit bit | DESIGNED |
   204	| K3 | apply | hit → one new expression (splice + level-order re-layout) | DESIGNED |
   205	| K4 | fold | closed subtree → literal | DESIGNED |
   206	| K5 | evaluate | expression × row → value | **BUILT** (`gpu_eval`) |
   207	| K6 | reduce | expression → 1−R² against its source's predictions | DESIGNED |
   208	| K7 | signature | expression → 16-row behavioural signature | **BUILT** on host (`chrom_score`) |
   209	
   210	A lint pass is exactly K1–K3: rule × node, bounded rounds, no global reasoning.
   211	
   212	- **K1** reuses K5's backward scan: a node's facts are a function of its symbol
   213	  (seed rows) and its children's facts (propagation rows). Caller facts
   214	  (`positive_vars`, `nonzero_vars`) are seed bits on variable nodes.
   215	- **K2** patterns are bounded depth (≤ 3): symbol id at the position; symbol
   216	  ids, wildcards and same-subtree constraints below; literal predicates
   217	  (`= 1.0`, `|k| < 1e-6`); required fact bits from K1. Same-subtree equality
   218	  (`Mul x x`) is a compare of two node ranges.
   219	- **K3** emits one output per hit. Two hits on one expression produce two
   220	  variants, never a merged rewrite. Re-layout is a BFS renumbering — the same
   221	  arity prefix sum as §3.
   222	- **K4 invariant: an input variable is never folded**, whatever its name (`c`
   223	  is a column before it is the speed of light). Inputs are an explicit
   224	  argument. Tests to carry over:
   225	  `smallest_form_folds_constants_but_never_an_input`,
   226	  `concretize_never_rewrites_an_input_variable`.
   227	- **K5** semantics are the engine's (protected ops, poison flag, trig cut-off
   228	  at 1e7) — the evaluator's contract, not the rewriter's.
   229	- **K6** compares against the *input expression's own* predictions, never an
   230	  extracted variant's. f32 on device; a chosen form is re-checked in f64 on the
   231	  host before it is returned. K6 is what licenses class-D rules (§5) and
   232	  nothing else needs it.
   233	
   234	## 5. Rule classes and termination (DESIGNED)
   235	
   236	Every rule declares a `rule_class`, checked when the table is loaded.
   237	
   238	Meaning-preserving (exact under the symbol definitions, NaN and ±inf included):
   239	
   240	```
   241	measure(expr) = (node_count, sum of depths of Neg nodes, ...)   lexicographic
   242	```
   243	
   244	- **A — shrinker**: strictly reduces `node_count`.
   245	- **B — enabler**: keeps `node_count`, strictly reduces the secondary measure
   246	  (the `sign` float-outs that move a `Neg` rootward so a class-A rule can
   247	  delete it; BUILT in egglog form).
   248	- anything expanding, bidirectional or commutative is **not admitted**. Those
   249	  families (distribute, trig expansion) stay in egglog on the CPU.
   250	
   251	"Shrink-only" would be wrong: fuller's own `sign` ruleset needs class B. The
   252	measure is what gives termination without an e-graph.
   253	
   254	Meaning-changing:
   255	
   256	- **D — data-justified**: not an equivalence. Admitted only when rows are
   257	  supplied, and only if K6's loss is within the caller's tolerance. Never fires
   258	  in fix mode without data.
   259	
   260	Fix mode uses A + B (+ D with rows). Generate mode uses A + B and keeps the
   261	intermediate forms rather than only the smallest.
   262	
   263	## 6. Algorithm (DESIGNED)
   264	
   265	```
   266	frontier = input batch
   267	repeat up to R rounds (R = 6, as SMALLEST_FORM_MAX_ROUNDS):
   268	    K1 facts -> K2 match -> K3 apply -> K4 fold
   269	    dedup by exact tokens, then by K7 signature
   270	    keep a beam of B per source expression (B = 8, as ECLASS_K)
   271	        fix mode:      smallest measure first
   272	        generate mode: smallest first, but structurally distinct forms preferred
   273	    stop when no expression produced a hit
   274	return per source: the beam, each with node_count and (if rows given) K6 loss
   275	```
   276	
   277	Host picks. Deterministic: ties broken by token order, never by arrival order.
   278	
   279	## 6a. Where it sits in the engine (DESIGNED)
   280	
   281	On the device the linter is not a side step; it is a stage of evaluation:
   282	
   283	```
   284	population -> K1 facts -> K2 match -> K3 apply -> K4 fold -> K5 evaluate -> scores
   285	```
   286	
   287	- **Every individual, every generation.** Today the expansion runs on the
   288	  subset an ORF cache lets through, returns through Python, and is re-encoded
   289	  into geppy tokens — where forms the primitive set cannot express are thrown
   290	  away (MEASURED: 2,619 in one 450-generation run).
   291	- **Meaning-preserving variants are not scored separately.** Class A and B
   292	  rewrites have identical behaviour, so identical error terms. Evaluate once
   293	  per individual, on its tidy form — the smallest, so also the cheapest
   294	  (MEASURED in the same run: 46,849 nodes removed by grafts; on the device that
   295	  is kernel work never done). Only class D variants (prune, snap) change the
   296	  function and need their own fitness.
   297	- **Tidy the phenotype always; the genotype by policy.** Evaluating the tidy
   298	  form is free and safe. Writing it back over the gene is a separate decision:
   299	  the untidy regions are the neutral material crossover and transposition draw
   300	  on, and grafting on ties was MEASURED to collapse diversity about 5x (hence
   301	  `GRAFT_MODE = improve`). The stage therefore emits two things per individual:
   302	  the tidy form (evaluation, reporting, "same model") and, under the graft
   303	  policy, a written-back gene. Baldwinian by default, Lamarckian by switch.
   304	
   305	## 7. What is lost against egglog
   306	
   307	- **Sharing.** An e-graph stores every equivalent form once; a beam stores B.
   308	  This costs generate mode variety.
   309	- **Grow-then-shrink.** A rewrite that must expand before it collapses
   310	  (`(a+b)^2 - a^2 - 2ab`) is unreachable by construction. This costs fix mode
   311	  reductions. The gate in §9 measures how many.
   312	- **Equality proofs.** egglog can decide whether two *given* forms are equal;
   313	  this only ever produces forms. **The engine never uses that.** When it needs
   314	  "are these the same" it asks the behavioural signature (K7). Only the parity
   315	  scorer proves equality, offline, and it stays on egglog. So this loss costs
   316	  the engine nothing.
   317	
   318	## 8. Multityped K-expressions (DESIGNED; depends on nucleotable)
   319	
   320	- Arity is per *instance*: with many-hot signatures the prefix sum needs the
   321	  arity this occurrence uses, resolved top-down from the root's required output
   322	  type. One extra pass, parallel per expression.
   323	- Patterns match `(semantic_id, signature)`. A template's output type must
   324	  equal the replaced subtree's — checked at table load.
   325	- The rewriter works on the expressed tree and never touches the tail. Writing
   326	  a result back into a fixed-length typed gene is nucleotable's open typed-tail
   327	  problem; this spec does not solve it. Class-A rules ease it (a smaller tree
   328	  fits the head it came from); class-B rules do not change size.
   329	- Nothing in §2–§6 is specific to floats. A different symbol table and rule
   330	  table make it a linter for boolean, string or mixed-type K-expressions. What
   331	  does not transfer is each domain's evaluator contract (K5) and guard facts.
   332	
   333	## 9. How rules are found, and the gate before any kernel
   334	
   335	**Lint survey (BUILT, run once).** Collect every hall-of-fame dump into one
   336	dataset (204 dumps, 847 unique expressions), count subtree shapes weighted by
   337	occurrence, and write rules for the shapes that survive the current linter.
   338	MEASURED: four agents over that dataset produced the `sign`, `is-nonneg` and
   339	reciprocal rules; weighted nodes after `smallest_form` went 154,554 → 150,926 (input
   340	161,408; 846 expressions), 155 smaller, none larger. The survey also showed what *not* to
   341	write: no Pythagorean or double-angle shape occurs in 847 expressions, and
   342	every rejected rule has its breaking input recorded in `docs/rule_proposals/`.
   343	Repeat the survey whenever a sweep adds material.
   344	
   345	**Gate.**
   346	
   347	1. Build the rule table and the §6 loop **on the CPU**, from the rules already
   348	   in `identities`, `powers`, `sign`, `rational` that pass §5.
   349	2. Run it over the 847 expressions with `examples/measure_smallest_form.rs`.
   350	3. Report **coverage** (share of egglog's weighted-node reduction reproduced),
   351	   **soundness** (K7 signature agreement with the input on every output),
   352	   **variety** in generate mode (distinct forms per input against egglog's
   353	   `extract_variants`), and **time** per expression.
   354	4. Kernels K1–K4 and K6 are built only if coverage justifies them. The CPU
   355	   prototype stays as the reference the kernels are checked against.
   356	
   357	## 10. Built today
   358	
   359	| Piece | Where |
   360	|---|---|
   361	| Level-order layout, evaluator kernel, 2-D dispatch | `src/gpu_eval.rs` |
   362	| Behavioural signature, train-only least squares | `src/chrom_score.rs` |
   363	| `semantic_id` symbol table, many-hot arity model | `src/karva.rs`, `src/geneframe.rs` |
   364	| Rules + guards as egglog text (the source for the rule table) | `src/ruleset/`, `src/expr.rs` |
   365	| Fix mode and generate mode on egglog | `src/extract.rs` (`smallest_form`, `denoise_candidates_assuming`) |
    23	/// The `sign` ruleset.
    24	pub const SIGN_RULESET: &str = r#"
    25	(ruleset sign)
    26	
    27	; ===== A. ABSORBERS — each strictly removes >= 1 node =====
    28	; A1  a - (-b) = a + b
    29	(rewrite (Sub a (Neg b)) (Add a b) :ruleset sign)
    30	; A2  a + (-b) = a - b ; (-a) + b = b - a
    31	(rewrite (Add a (Neg b)) (Sub a b) :ruleset sign)
    32	(rewrite (Add (Neg a) b) (Sub b a) :ruleset sign)
    33	; A3  -(a - b) = b - a
    34	(rewrite (Neg (Sub a b)) (Sub b a) :ruleset sign)
    35	; A4  0 - x = -x
    36	(rewrite (Sub (Num 0.0) x) (Neg x) :ruleset sign)
    37	; A5  -(literal) folds ; -(c + x) = (-c) - x, both Add orders
    38	(rewrite (Neg (Num a)) (Num (neg a)) :ruleset sign)
    39	(rewrite (Neg (Add (Num c) x)) (Sub (Num (neg c)) x) :ruleset sign)
    40	(rewrite (Neg (Add x (Num c))) (Sub (Num (neg c)) x) :ruleset sign)
    41	; A6  sign pairs cancel through a product / quotient (incl. protected divide)
    42	(rewrite (Mul (Neg a) (Neg b)) (Mul a b) :ruleset sign)
    43	(rewrite (Div (Neg a) (Neg b)) (Div a b) :ruleset sign)
    44	(rewrite (ProtectedDiv (Neg a) (Neg b)) (ProtectedDiv a b) :ruleset sign)
    45	(rule ((= e (ProtectedDiv (Num c) (Neg b))) (< c 0.0))
    46	      ((union e (ProtectedDiv (Num (neg c)) b))) :ruleset sign)
    47	(rule ((= e (Div (Num c) (Neg b))) (< c 0.0))
    48	      ((union e (Div (Num (neg c)) b))) :ruleset sign)
    49	; A7  a negation meeting a difference inside a product flips the difference
    50	(rewrite (Neg (Mul (Sub a b) c)) (Mul (Sub b a) c) :ruleset sign)
    51	(rewrite (Neg (Mul c (Sub a b))) (Mul c (Sub b a)) :ruleset sign)
    52	(rewrite (Mul (Sub a b) (Neg c)) (Mul (Sub b a) c) :ruleset sign)
    53	(rewrite (Mul (Neg c) (Sub a b)) (Mul c (Sub b a)) :ruleset sign)
    54	; A8  EVEN functions swallow a Neg / an Abs
    55	(rewrite (Pow2 (Neg x)) (Pow2 x) :ruleset sign)
    56	(rewrite (Pow2 (Abs x)) (Pow2 x) :ruleset sign)
    57	(rewrite (Abs (Neg x)) (Abs x) :ruleset sign)
    58	(rewrite (Cos (Neg x)) (Cos x) :ruleset sign)
    59	(rewrite (Cos (Abs x)) (Cos x) :ruleset sign)
    60	(rewrite (ProtectedSqrt (Neg x)) (ProtectedSqrt x) :ruleset sign)
    61	(rewrite (ProtectedSqrt (Abs x)) (ProtectedSqrt x) :ruleset sign)
    62	(rewrite (ProtectedLog (Neg x)) (ProtectedLog x) :ruleset sign)
    63	(rewrite (ProtectedLog (Abs x)) (ProtectedLog x) :ruleset sign)
    64	; A9  Abs of a function whose range is already >= 0 (or NaN)
    65	(rewrite (Abs (Pow2 x)) (Pow2 x) :ruleset sign)
    66	(rewrite (Abs (Sqrt x)) (Sqrt x) :ruleset sign)
    67	(rewrite (Abs (ProtectedSqrt x)) (ProtectedSqrt x) :ruleset sign)
    68	(rewrite (Abs (ProtectedExp x)) (ProtectedExp x) :ruleset sign)
    69	
    70	; ===== B. FLOAT-OUTS — size-neutral; move a Neg one level ROOTWARD so an
    71	; absorber above can delete it. Never push a Neg down. =====
    72	; B1  (-a) - b = -(a + b)
    73	(rewrite (Sub (Neg a) b) (Neg (Add a b)) :ruleset sign)
    74	; B2  Neg out of a product / quotient (raw and protected divide)
    75	(rewrite (Mul (Neg a) b) (Neg (Mul a b)) :ruleset sign)
    76	(rewrite (Mul a (Neg b)) (Neg (Mul a b)) :ruleset sign)
    77	(rewrite (Div (Neg a) b) (Neg (Div a b)) :ruleset sign)
    78	(rewrite (Div a (Neg b)) (Neg (Div a b)) :ruleset sign)
    79	(rewrite (ProtectedDiv (Neg a) b) (Neg (ProtectedDiv a b)) :ruleset sign)
    80	(rewrite (ProtectedDiv a (Neg b)) (Neg (ProtectedDiv a b)) :ruleset sign)
    81	; B3  ODD functions pass a Neg through (raw Inv only — NOT ProtectedInv)
    82	(rewrite (Sin (Neg x)) (Neg (Sin x)) :ruleset sign)
    83	(rewrite (Tan (Neg x)) (Neg (Tan x)) :ruleset sign)
    84	(rewrite (Tanh (Neg x)) (Neg (Tanh x)) :ruleset sign)
    85	(rewrite (Pow3 (Neg x)) (Neg (Pow3 x)) :ruleset sign)
    86	(rewrite (Inv (Neg x)) (Neg (Inv x)) :ruleset sign)
    87	"#;
