   446	/// Like [`terms_to_karva`] but accepts an optional `target_head_length`.
   447	///
   448	/// GEP requires a uniform `head_length` across all genes in a chromosome, so a
   449	/// rewritten term whose natural head is shorter than the chromosome's configured
   450	/// head breaks geppy's mating ops when grafted back. When `target_head_length`
   451	/// is set:
   452	///
   453	/// * shorter natural head → extend it to the target with terminal filler. Those
   454	///   extra head slots sit beyond the live k-expression's BFS frontier, so the
   455	///   level-order decoder never visits them — the decoded tree (hence the
   456	///   semantics) is unchanged. This is the same syntactic position geppy fills
   457	///   with random tokens at random-init time.
   458	/// * longer natural head → return `oversized = true`; the caller must drop the
   459	///   candidate. We never truncate, because that would change the term.
   460	/// * exact / `None` → unchanged behaviour.
