     1	# SPEC: fuller on the GPU — a graph linter for K-expressions
     2	
     3	Version 2 · 2026-09-20 · supersedes v1 (commit `85059b8`)
     4	
     5	Status tags: **BUILT** (running, tested) · **MEASURED** (number from a real
     6	run) · **DESIGNED** (specified here, not implemented).
     7	
     8	Scope: fuller only. nucleotable appears as the *shape of the inputs* (symbol
     9	table, typed signatures), nothing more. The measurements that motivate this are
    10	in `CR_fuller_wgpu_port.md`: at the 64 rows the join sends, fuller's data
    11	scoring is not worth a kernel (MEASURED: 0.16 s per 847 expressions), and
    12	egglog cannot be moved. A GPU fuller **is** the rewriter below — a new build,
    13	not a port.
    14	
    15	Changes from v1: §1 says what the thing *is* (v1 only said how it works); the
    16	rule classes in §5 now separate meaning-preserving fixes from data-justified
    17	ones; §7 is corrected — the engine never used equality proofs, so that loss
    18	costs it nothing; §9 adds the lint survey as the way rules are found.
    19	
    20	---
    21	
    22	## 1. What it is
    23	
    24	**A graph rewriting tool: a linter, with autofix, for K-expressions.**
    25	
    26	| Linter | fuller |
    27	|---|---|
    28	| a rule: "this shape, fix it like this" | `Sub a (Neg b) -> Add a b` |
    29	| autofix must not change behaviour | every rule carries a soundness argument against the symbol's definition; `ProtectedDiv(x,x) -> 1` was refused because it breaks at x = 0 |
    30	| rules are written against a language definition | rules reference symbols by `semantic_id` (§2) |
    31	| rule sets are chosen per project | usable rules are derived from the kingdom (§2) |
    32	| flow / type facts ("never null here") | guard facts (`is-nonneg`, `is-nonzero`) (§4, K1) |
    33	| survey a codebase, write rules for what fires | the hall-of-fame census (§9) |
    34	
    35	It works on **meaning**, where GEP's own operators work on **tokens**. It has
    36	two modes and one extension:
    37	
    38	1. **Fix mode** — one input, one output: the smallest equivalent writing.
    39	   `smallest_form` (BUILT, egglog). Used for reporting, for exactness against
    40	   the truth, and with the behavioural signature for "the same model".
    41	2. **Generate mode** — one input, *k* outputs: different writings of the same
    42	   function. The e-class expansion (BUILT, egglog). This is not mutation in the
    43	   GEP sense — the phenotype does not change. It is a *constructed* neutral
    44	   variation: the graft changes nothing about an individual's fitness and
    45	   everything about what crossover, transposition and point mutation can do to
    46	   it next. GEP gets this by luck through its neutral regions; here it is done
    47	   by algebra.
    48	3. **Data-justified fixes** — prune, additive strip, snap. These *do* change
    49	   the function, by an amount the rows cannot see (`1.00000000451 -> 1`, a term
    50	   whose removal moves nothing). They are not equivalences; without rows they
    51	   are refused (BUILT: `prune_on_data` returns None on no data). A profile-
    52	   guided optimiser, not a linter.
    53	
    54	A linter returns one fix because its consumer is a person. This returns a
    55	neighbourhood because its consumer is an evolutionary search.
    56	
    57	## 2. Tables (DESIGNED; `symbols` is the nucleotable schema)
    58	
    59	The symbol table is **nucleotable's `symbols` table, column for column**
    60	(`nucleotable/db/schema.py`), so the linter is general to any kingdom that
    61	schema can describe — not to the float algebra fuller happens to serve today.
    62	fuller already holds it in this shape (`src/geneframe.rs`: `Symbol`, `Arity`,
    63	`SymbolTable`, `master_table()`, BUILT).
    64	
    65	```
    66	symbols(                          -- nucleotable schema, unchanged
    67	    kingdom, symbol,              -- key; symbol > 0 function, < 0 terminal
    68	    symbol_name, alias,
    69	    in_S, in_I, in_F, in_B, in_A, in_L,        -- typed many-hot input arity
    70	    out_S, out_I, out_F, out_B, out_A, out_L,  -- typed output arity
    71	    semantic_id                   -- fuller's one addition: the MEANING
    72	)
    73	rules(rule_id PK, pattern, template, guard, rule_class, family)
    74	rule_symbols(rule_id FK, semantic_id FK)         -- every id on EITHER side
    75	guard_seeds(semantic_id FK, fact)                -- Pow2 -> nonneg
    76	guard_propagation(semantic_id FK, child_facts, fact)   -- Mul: nonneg,nonneg -> nonneg
    77	```
    78	
    79	- **Why `semantic_id` and not `(kingdom, symbol)`.** `(kingdom, symbol)` says
    80	  where a symbol sits in a geneframe; `semantic_id` says what it computes. The
    81	  same meaning appears in many kingdoms under different integers and aliases.
    82	  Rules and guards key on `semantic_id`, so one rule serves every kingdom that
    83	  contains that meaning, and a kingdom is still just a query over `symbols`.
    84	  This is the one column the linter asks nucleotable to carry.
    85	- **Arity is read from the typed columns**, never stored separately: a
    86	  single-type symbol's arity is its one non-zero `in_*`; a many-hot symbol has
    87	  several, resolved per instance (§8). Pattern positions and templates carry
    88	  the same `in_*`/`out_*` signature, so a rule is type-checked against the
    89	  symbol rows when the tables load.
    90	- The evaluator's opcode is not a schema column: it is
    91	  the evaluator's own map from `semantic_id` to a kernel case (`Op::from_math`,
    92	  BUILT). A `semantic_id` with no kernel case can be linted but not scored, so
    93	  class D rules are unavailable for it.
    94	
    95	- **A rule is a theorem about specific symbols.** `x / 1 -> x` holds for
    96	  `ProtectedDiv` only because that symbol is defined as "0 when |b| < 1e-6,
    97	  else a/b". So a rule has a dependency on the symbol table, and
    98	  `rule_symbols` records it: every symbol the pattern **and the template**
    99	  mention (`Div 1 x -> Inv x` depends on `Inv` too).
   100	- **That dependency is a join.** For a kingdom `K` (a query over `symbols`), a
   101	  rule is usable iff none of its `rule_symbols` rows points outside `K` —
   102	  relational division, written as an anti-join. The ruleset is *derived* from
   103	  the kingdom the way the kingdom is derived from the master table.
   104	- **Identity is meaning.** Keys are `semantic_id`, never names (BUILT in
   105	  `karva.rs`). `Div` and `ProtectedDiv` are different ids, so a rule for one
   106	  cannot fire on the other. **A changed definition is a new `semantic_id`** —
   107	  moving ProtectedDiv's threshold under the same id would silently falsify
   108	  every rule that references it.
   109	- Guards are rows too: a seed is a fact about a symbol; propagation is a fact
   110	  about a symbol given its children's facts.
   111	
   112	What the kingdom join does **not** do: it does not make rules confluent.
   113	Non-confluence is two usable rules undoing each other (distribute + trig), not
   114	a membership problem. Termination comes from §5.
   115	
   116	## 2b. General by design, Symbolic Regression by build
   117	
   118	The design carries the whole nucleotable schema. The build and its tests cover
   119	one kingdom: **Symbolic Regression**. REGEX is the intended second kingdom,
   120	later; nothing below may be written in a way that has to be undone for it.
   121	
   122	General from the first commit — these cost nothing extra now and are expensive
   123	to retrofit:
   124	
   125	| Piece | How it stays general |
   126	|---|---|
   127	| table formats | `symbols` is the full schema: every `in_*` / `out_*` column is read, not just `in_F` / `out_F`. New type columns (NLP, Botji) are more columns, not new code. |
   128	| keys | rules and guards key on `semantic_id`; expressions carry `(kingdom, symbol)`; the join between them is data. |
   129	| rule rows | every pattern position and template carries a type signature, even though in SR every one is `F -> F`. The load-time type check runs on SR rules too, where it trivially passes. |
   130	| kingdom filter | usable rules are derived by the anti-join, even with one kingdom loaded. |
   131	| node layout | a node stores `symbol` and its resolved arity; the arity comes from the typed columns via one function, which for SR returns `in_F`. |
   132	| K1–K4 | no float assumptions: matching, splicing, re-layout and the ordering measure work on symbol ids and arities only. |
   133	| literals | a literal node carries a type tag and a payload slot. SR uses the float payload; a string payload is an index into a side table, reserved, not built. |
   134	
   135	Symbolic Regression only, for now — each is a named seam, not a hidden assumption:
   136	
   137	| Piece | SR-only today | What REGEX would need |
   138	|---|---|---|
   139	| K5 evaluate | float kernel, protected-op contract, f32 | its own evaluator; strings are not a WGSL type, so likely host-side |
   140	| K6 / class D rules | 1−R² against the source's predictions | a loss over string outputs (match agreement on example rows) |
   141	| K7 signature | 16 float rows | 16 example strings -> hash of outputs |
   142	| K4 fold | evaluates a closed float subtree | a closed string subtree through its evaluator |
   143	| guard facts | `is-positive`, `is-nonzero`, `is-nonneg` | its own facts (`is-anchored`, `never-empty`, ...) — rows in the same two tables |
   144	| rule content | `identities`, `powers`, `sign`, `rational` | its own rows; `concat` rules would already be shared with SQL |
   145	| arity resolution | single type, so arity = `in_F` | per-instance, top-down from the root's output type (§8) |
   146	| ordering measure | `(node_count, Neg depth)` | `node_count` is general; the secondary measure is per rule family |
   147	
   148	So a second kingdom is: rows in five tables, one evaluator, one loss, one
   149	signature. If adding REGEX needs a change to K1–K3 or to the table formats,
   150	this section was wrong and should be corrected first.
   151	
   152	**Tests.** The build is tested on SR only (the 847-expression dataset, §9).
   153	One structural test guards generality without a second evaluator: load the
   154	REGEX and SQL seed rows from nucleotable alongside SR and assert that the
   155	kingdom filter hands SR exactly the SR-usable rules, that a `concat` rule is
   156	offered to REGEX and SQL and never to SR, and that a rule with a mistyped
   157	template is refused at load.
   158	
   159	## 2a. Interface (DESIGNED)
   160	
   161	```
   162	lint(
   163	    symbols,            -- nucleotable schema + semantic_id (§2)          (the kingdom)
   164	    rules,              -- pattern, template, guard, rule_class, family
   165	    rule_symbols,       -- rule_id -> semantic_id, both sides of the rule
   166	    guard_seeds,        -- semantic_id -> fact
   167	    guard_propagation,  -- semantic_id + child facts -> fact
   168	    expressions,        -- the batch of K-expressions (level-order arrays)
   169	    inputs,             -- which names are data columns (never folded)
   170	    caller_facts,       -- positive / nonzero variables
   171	    rows = none,        -- optional data; enables class D (prune, snap)
   172	    mode = fix | generate,
   173	    rounds = 6, beam = 8, tolerance
   174	) -> per expression: [ (K-expression, node_count, loss if rows) ... ]
   175	```
   176	
   177	- **Once per kingdom:** the five tables. The usable-rule anti-join (§2) and
   178	  the rule-class check (§5) run at load; the result stays resident on the
   179	  device, as the dataset does today (`GpuEvaluator`, BUILT).
   180	- **Per call:** `expressions`, `caller_facts`.
